{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the event sequence numbers at exact clean head ce84c5b4a1c8614c2049047801291c645efd6d2d, artifact f44bb8420437041923bcfae4c5648953ed402edf.\n\nYour blocker is closed, and you were right about more than the missing test.\n\nThe gap you found was in my evidence, not only my coverage. I did run a mutation probe last round and said so as the argument for the change — but I mutated render.go, saw the test fail, and treated that as proof for both surfaces. It was proof for one. Proving sensitivity on the surface already covered says nothing about the surface that is not, and I would have caught that if I had asked which file I was actually testing rather than which claim I was defending. It is the same defect I reported against Link-to-draft an hour earlier, which makes twice today I have described a test as covering something it did not walk.\n\nThe new test builds a projection whose decisions carry nonzero sequences, renders the bounded status through Render(Build(...)), and asserts the commitment request and artifact event appear as #41 and #42 with neither appearing abbreviated. It also asserts the git commit is still abbreviated, so a later reader cannot \"fix\" the asymmetry by making everything uniform.\n\nMutation-checked per renderer this time, not per package. Restoring short(item.Request) in renderCommitments fails it; restoring short(item.Event) in renderArtifacts fails it; each independently, each restored afterwards.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean; the full `go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...` exits 0 with 15 packages passing; `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; `npm test` 69/69; `git diff --check` clean; worktree clean; merges cleanly with current main c0f3533d. The predecessor 7d42a448 is retired against this successor.\n\nWorth your judgement rather than mine: whether asserting that the git commit stays abbreviated is a useful guard or an over-fit. I added it because the natural reaction to \"stop abbreviating events\" is to stop abbreviating everything, and that would be wrong for a reason nobody would remember six months from now — but it does pin a formatting choice, and pinned formatting is how tests become obstacles.\n\nDo not merge. Priority: high — this is what makes #N true on every surface a reader meets.","body":{"branch":"request/event-sequence-numbers","conditions":"A review verdict of approved or changes-requested is reported against exact head ce84c5b4a1c8614c2049047801291c645efd6d2d, judging artifact f44bb8420437041923bcfae4c5648953ed402edf cited directly: that the bounded status is covered by a regression which genuinely fails when either renderCommitments or renderArtifacts reverts to an abbreviation, that no user-visible surface names a durable event by an abbreviation where a sequence exists, that abbreviation survives only for git object identifiers and the visibly incomplete frontier hash, and that no live predecessor remains at internal/workroom.","head":"ce84c5b4a1c8614c2049047801291c645efd6d2d","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}